@startuml fpu-enable
' The two switches of an FPU, and what happens when only one is on.
' Grounded in Src/core.c, Inc/core.h, Inc/registers.h (SCB_CPACR),
' Src/main.c (SystemInit) and Startup/startup_stm32l476rgtx.s.

title FPU: two switches, only one of them is a compiler flag

skinparam shadowing false

rectangle "Switch 1: BUILD TIME" as Build #ddeeff {
  card "-mfloat-abi=hard\n-mfpu=fpv4-sp-d16" as Flags
  note bottom of Flags
    Tells the COMPILER an FPU exists.
    Float code becomes VFP instructions
    (vmul.f32, vadd.f32, ...).
    Was already on in this project.
  end note
}

rectangle "Switch 2: RUN TIME" as Run #ddffdd {
  card "CPACR at 0xE000ED88\nCP10 + CP11 full access" as Cpacr
  note bottom of Cpacr
    Grants the CORE access to the FPU.
    Resets to "denied", so the FPU is
    OFF after every reset.
    This is what was missing.
  end note
}

rectangle "What used to happen" as Bad #ffdddd {
  card "float x = a * b;" as Float
  card "vmul.f32 hits a disabled unit" as Vmul
  card "UsageFault, NOCP bit" as Fault
  card "escalates to HardFault" as Hard
  card "Default_Handler:\n  b Infinite_Loop" as Loop
  card "the board freezes,\nnothing on the serial line" as Dead

  Float --> Vmul
  Vmul  --> Fault
  Fault --> Hard
  Hard  --> Loop
  Loop  --> Dead
}

Flags --> Float : compiles silently
Cpacr -[#green,bold]-> Vmul : with switch 2 on,\nthis path never starts

note as Order
  **Order of bring-up**
  startup .s sets the stack pointer
  -> bl SystemInit
       -> core_enable_fpu()
            write CPACR, then DSB, then ISB
  -> copy .data, zero .bss
  -> main()

  Earliest possible point, before any C
  code could execute a float.
end note

note as Silent
  **Why nothing caught it**
  The compiler cannot warn: it was told
  an FPU exists. The linker cannot see it.
  Host tests pass, because a PC has a
  working FPU. CI passes for the same
  reason. Only the board fails, and it
  fails by going quiet.
end note

@enduml
